1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It can connect you to anybody, anywhere,

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
through a network that spans the globe.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Steeped in controversy when invented,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,000
it's now a lifeline.

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
And what is your emergency?

6
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
An entertainment hub,

7
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
and an unparalleled tool for human interaction.

8
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Hi, Brian.

9
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
But privacy isn't assured.

10
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, The Telephone on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Over the past 130 years, we've seen desk phones, pay phones, and mobile phones, rotary

12
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:55,032
phones, push-button phones, and phones with no dials or buttons at all.

13
00:00:55,033 --> 00:00:56,400
Fat phones, thin

14
00:00:56,401 --> 00:01:02,400
phones, and even kids' phones. But no matter the shape, size,

15
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:06,800
color, or configuration, every phone has to be connected to a

16
00:01:06,801 --> 00:01:11,040
network. Today, the world's largest network

17
00:01:11,041 --> 00:01:15,560
is operated by AT&T. The company's Global Network

18
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:19,320
Operations Center, or GNOC, is located in Bedminster, New

19
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:23,920
Jersey. The center's 144 screens display

20
00:01:23,921 --> 00:01:27,520
data representing the flow of voice and information traffic.

21
00:01:27,521 --> 00:01:31,360
This traffic is being routed to and from AT&T subscribers in

22
00:01:31,361 --> 00:01:35,660
240 different countries. This is the nerve center of

23
00:01:35,661 --> 00:01:40,580
the AT&T network, a 200,000 square foot facility that's

24
00:01:40,581 --> 00:01:45,580
designed to monitor and manage the AT&T networks worldwide.

25
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:50,680
When a natural disaster occurs or a major world event is in

26
00:01:50,681 --> 00:01:55,680
is in the making, or even your favorite TV reality show is

27
00:01:55,780 --> 00:02:01,120
having a big voting night, that affects communication traffic.

28
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:03,460
And that's what we do here in this facility.

29
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,600
We watch those events and we take action where necessary

30
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:10,900
to ensure our customers' communications are always

31
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,840
satisfactory and are always completed.

32
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:18,300
Network management technicians can anticipate

33
00:02:18,301 --> 00:02:23,540
bottlenecks and reroute call traffic around them.

34
00:02:23,541 --> 00:02:27,100
The information monitored in the GNOC is piped in from more than

35
00:02:27,101 --> 00:02:31,340
5,000 central switching offices that AT&T operates around the

36
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:34,940
world. One such central office is known

37
00:02:34,941 --> 00:02:39,020
as SF21, located in the south of Market District in San

38
00:02:39,021 --> 00:02:44,060
Francisco. Each day, 10 million calls to and

39
00:02:44,061 --> 00:02:47,500
from AT&T subscribers are connected through banks of

40
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:51,740
switches, known as a DMS. All of our calls route

41
00:02:51,741 --> 00:02:56,240
through a digital multiplex system, a DMS switch, and the

42
00:02:56,241 --> 00:03:00,740
calls go into those switches and out to the world, serving all of

43
00:03:00,741 --> 00:03:04,260
our customers. When a typical AT&T

44
00:03:04,261 --> 00:03:08,660
customer serviced by SF21 places a call, the transmission flows

45
00:03:08,661 --> 00:03:11,660
out of their home or business along a pair of thin copper

46
00:03:11,661 --> 00:03:16,660
wires to a trunk line. The trunk line then feeds into

47
00:03:16,661 --> 00:03:22,160
into the central office. The trunk lines enter the building

48
00:03:22,161 --> 00:03:25,500
in a secure, climate-controlled subterranean room called the

49
00:03:25,501 --> 00:03:29,900
cable vault. The orange conduit contains

50
00:03:29,901 --> 00:03:34,120
our very high-capacity fiber optic cables coming in and out

51
00:03:34,121 --> 00:03:37,620
of the central office, but the vast majority of our calls that

52
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:41,060
come into the central office come in on the large copper

53
00:03:41,061 --> 00:03:44,060
distribution cables. In this case, this is a 3,600-pair

54
00:03:44,061 --> 00:03:48,700
pair copper cable, much like those used at the time of

55
00:03:48,701 --> 00:03:51,800
Alexander Graham Bell. The technology has not changed.

56
00:03:51,801 --> 00:03:56,900
The 3,600 pair cable is split into 36 100 pair cables that

57
00:03:56,901 --> 00:03:59,600
travel up to the main distributing frame on the next

58
00:03:59,601 --> 00:04:03,020
floor. At the main

59
00:04:03,021 --> 00:04:06,760
distributing frame, the 100 wire cables are broken back out into

60
00:04:06,761 --> 00:04:13,020
individual customer lines, known as a twisted pair.

61
00:04:13,021 --> 00:04:18,020
From there, the call is then routed to the DMS switches.

62
00:04:18,021 --> 00:04:21,220
What we've got here are the individual line cards.

63
00:04:21,221 --> 00:04:24,520
Each one of these represents an AT&T customer line,

64
00:04:24,521 --> 00:04:27,120
which is connected from the main distribution frame

65
00:04:27,121 --> 00:04:28,620
to the line card.

66
00:04:28,621 --> 00:04:31,320
Here, the analog to digital conversion takes place,

67
00:04:31,321 --> 00:04:33,920
and the digital signal gets sent out on the fiber optic

68
00:04:33,921 --> 00:04:37,020
cables to the world.

69
00:04:37,021 --> 00:04:41,620
Fiber optic cable was invented in 1970 by Corning Glass.

70
00:04:41,621 --> 00:04:44,180
By converting an analog or digital signal into a

71
00:04:44,181 --> 00:04:47,460
corresponding optical signal, it can then be sent through a

72
00:04:47,461 --> 00:04:50,260
fiber optic strand at near the speed of light.

73
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:56,980
A call sent out of a central office can take a variety of

74
00:04:56,981 --> 00:05:01,060
paths, depending on its final destination.

75
00:05:01,061 --> 00:05:04,900
These include long-distance landlines or microwave

76
00:05:04,901 --> 00:05:09,620
transmitters in areas that aren't connected by landlines.

77
00:05:09,621 --> 00:05:13,320
If the call is going overseas, it can be beamed off a satellite

78
00:05:13,321 --> 00:05:17,720
or routed through an undersea cable.

79
00:05:17,721 --> 00:05:20,120
The way a call knows how to get where it's going

80
00:05:20,121 --> 00:05:22,760
is encoded in the telephone number, which consists

81
00:05:22,761 --> 00:05:26,080
of three parts, the area code, the prefix,

82
00:05:26,081 --> 00:05:31,680
and the subscriber number. The area code

83
00:05:31,681 --> 00:05:33,580
tells the switch which long-distance line

84
00:05:33,581 --> 00:05:36,820
to put the call on. The three-digit prefix

85
00:05:36,821 --> 00:05:40,060
routes the call to an individual's central office.

86
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:42,820
A central office switch reads the four-digit subscriber

87
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:45,760
number, then routes the call to the corresponding home

88
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:49,660
or business. Today, we take direct dialing

89
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:52,760
for granted. But early phones didn't have

90
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:56,180
dials or keypads. Calls had to be hand routed by

91
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:59,680
human operators in offices known as exchanges.

92
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:02,420
They had to tell the operator who they wanted to talk to.

93
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:04,620
The operator would make the decision whether this would be

94
00:06:04,621 --> 00:06:07,260
call, they could handle it locally, or if it was a long

95
00:06:07,261 --> 00:06:10,060
distance call, and it would go through a central office switch

96
00:06:10,061 --> 00:06:12,220
with a large bank of switchboards and operators

97
00:06:12,221 --> 00:06:17,260
connecting the line. In the late teens, the

98
00:06:17,261 --> 00:06:20,140
addition of rotary dials to telephones meant that operators

99
00:06:20,141 --> 00:06:22,940
started to be replaced by electromechanical switching

100
00:06:22,941 --> 00:06:26,940
equipment, which uses motors, rods, and relays.

101
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:33,580
The Museum of Telecommunications in Seattle,

102
00:06:33,581 --> 00:06:37,720
in Seattle, Washington, houses 11,000 square feet of vintage

103
00:06:37,721 --> 00:06:41,320
electromechanical switching equipment.

104
00:06:41,321 --> 00:06:44,640
Thanks to a team of retired telephone company technicians,

105
00:06:44,641 --> 00:06:49,620
all of it still works, including this early electromechanical

106
00:06:49,621 --> 00:06:53,340
switch called a step-by-step.

107
00:06:53,341 --> 00:06:57,320
Each one of these switches will respond to what you dial

108
00:06:57,321 --> 00:06:59,440
on your telephone dial.

109
00:06:59,441 --> 00:07:03,540
If you dial a 9, you would step up to 9th level,

110
00:07:03,541 --> 00:07:07,780
So it's a mechanical switch that responds to your dial and then

111
00:07:07,781 --> 00:07:11,540
seeks a path for you to complete the call.

112
00:07:11,541 --> 00:07:15,280
By 1999, all US phone companies had replaced their

113
00:07:15,281 --> 00:07:18,280
electromechanical switches with digital versions.

114
00:07:18,281 --> 00:07:23,500
For the first 100 years of telephony, telephones had to be

115
00:07:23,501 --> 00:07:27,800
physically connected to the switches through miles of wire.

116
00:07:27,801 --> 00:07:32,500
But in the 1980s, the telephone shed its copper tether, and

117
00:07:32,501 --> 00:07:37,900
telecommunications went mobile. Today, there are more than 200

118
00:07:37,901 --> 00:07:41,900
million mobile phone users in the United States and 2 billion

119
00:07:41,901 --> 00:07:46,500
worldwide. The precursor to today's mobile

120
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:50,900
phone was the car phone. Early car phones relied on large

121
00:07:50,901 --> 00:07:54,900
mobile radios with processors called logic units that filled

122
00:07:54,901 --> 00:07:58,900
the trunk of a car. In the 1970s, Robert Miller was a

123
00:07:58,901 --> 00:08:03,380
Robert Miller was a Bell Labs researcher working on mobile phone technology.

124
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:13,800
I think the future became clear to me one day when one of the individuals at Bell Labs

125
00:08:13,801 --> 00:08:20,960
who was charged with the design of the so-called logic unit came running into the laboratory and said,

126
00:08:20,961 --> 00:08:34,961
I just heard that a new company called Intel has just put out a microcomputer called an 8008 that could do everything in this entire logic unit.

127
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:38,280
And we were transfixed.

128
00:08:38,680 --> 00:08:40,280
That moment was an epiphany.

129
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:41,780
It changed everything.

130
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:49,780
That epiphany gave birth to the first true portable phone, the Motorola Dynatac 8000X.

131
00:08:49,781 --> 00:08:52,780
Known as the BRIC because of its heft and size,

132
00:08:52,781 --> 00:08:57,780
the Dynatac retailed for a mere $3,995,

133
00:08:57,781 --> 00:09:01,780
plus the monthly service fee.

134
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:03,780
A new type of network had to be created

135
00:09:03,781 --> 00:09:05,780
to connect mobile phones.

136
00:09:05,781 --> 00:09:09,780
It was called cellular technology.

137
00:09:09,781 --> 00:09:13,780
A cellular system peppers a town with several small antennas.

138
00:09:13,781 --> 00:09:15,780
Each service is an area, or cell,

139
00:09:15,781 --> 00:09:18,780
of up to eight miles in radius.

140
00:09:18,781 --> 00:09:22,780
When a wireless customer moves from one cell to another,

141
00:09:22,781 --> 00:09:25,780
computers automatically switch the call to the next nearest

142
00:09:25,781 --> 00:09:30,780
cell antenna. Cell phone towers are now

143
00:09:30,781 --> 00:09:34,780
common features of our landscape. Because of some local zoning

144
00:09:34,781 --> 00:09:38,780
laws, wireless providers like Singular design what are called

145
00:09:38,781 --> 00:09:42,780
stealth sites. Some don't look like cell towers

146
00:09:42,781 --> 00:09:45,780
at all. Despite their disguises,

147
00:09:45,781 --> 00:09:49,180
disguises, all cell towers work the same way.

148
00:09:49,181 --> 00:09:51,880
When a singular customer makes a phone call, they press send on

149
00:09:51,881 --> 00:09:55,480
their mobile handset. That call then goes over radio

150
00:09:55,481 --> 00:09:58,080
frequencies to the nearest cell site.

151
00:09:58,081 --> 00:10:02,180
The cell site takes that signal and sends it back to an MTSO,

152
00:10:02,181 --> 00:10:05,480
or Mobile Telephony Switching Center.

153
00:10:05,481 --> 00:10:08,880
An MTSO, like this one operated by Singular in the Los

154
00:10:08,881 --> 00:10:12,180
Angeles area, is the wireless equivalent of the landline's

155
00:10:12,181 --> 00:10:16,320
central office. Here, calls are processed by banks

156
00:10:16,321 --> 00:10:22,180
of digital switches, which route them to their final destinations.

157
00:10:22,181 --> 00:10:25,940
Today's cell phones are sleek and full of features, but that

158
00:10:25,941 --> 00:10:28,240
certainly wasn't the case when they first hit the market in

159
00:10:28,241 --> 00:10:32,180
the 1980s. This is a chronological display

160
00:10:32,181 --> 00:10:35,440
of the miniaturization of cell phones, starting with the

161
00:10:35,441 --> 00:10:38,620
largest here, the bag phone. It's a Tough Talker, which was

162
00:10:38,621 --> 00:10:43,840
the name of it, and it provides, to today's standard, very little memory, very little

163
00:10:43,841 --> 00:10:48,380
features, but it was a very durable device and was very successful in its time.

164
00:10:48,381 --> 00:10:52,820
This one is considered the first flip phone.

165
00:10:52,821 --> 00:10:54,920
This is a DPC-500.

166
00:10:54,921 --> 00:11:02,300
This phone has more memory, smaller circuit board inside, it offers a different display

167
00:11:02,301 --> 00:11:07,280
and certainly a much more user-friendly feel.

168
00:11:07,281 --> 00:11:10,400
This unit here is considered what's now a candy bar phone.

169
00:11:10,401 --> 00:11:14,400
It has a pop-up antenna, built-in battery in the back.

170
00:11:14,401 --> 00:11:20,840
We then moved on to an even smaller, lighter, highly featured phone, considered a 3035.

171
00:11:20,841 --> 00:11:27,140
This model had a lithium-ion battery, very, very lightweight, much larger display, color

172
00:11:27,141 --> 00:11:28,140
display.

173
00:11:28,141 --> 00:11:30,800
Then we have the V3.

174
00:11:30,801 --> 00:11:34,040
This featured phone has tremendous talk time and memory.

175
00:11:34,041 --> 00:11:38,680
You could have pictures on here, video on here, and is extremely

176
00:11:38,681 --> 00:11:44,440
lightweight and extremely thin. As today's mobile devices become

177
00:11:44,441 --> 00:11:48,440
more like handheld computers than telephones, the miniaturization

178
00:11:48,441 --> 00:11:55,360
trend is reversing. In business since 1986, cellular

179
00:11:55,361 --> 00:11:58,480
fantasy in Santa Monica, California, has witnessed the

180
00:11:58,481 --> 00:12:02,720
cell phone revolution firsthand. Before it was simply for

181
00:12:02,721 --> 00:12:06,420
Now we have text messaging, we have email, MP3 players,

182
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:09,460
watching videos on the phone, live TV, music.

183
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:13,560
Everything's being compacted into this one mobile device.

184
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:16,060
With more advanced devices arriving daily,

185
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:19,480
the average wireless customer replaces his or her cell phone

186
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:21,880
every 18 months.

187
00:12:21,980 --> 00:12:25,680
So what happens to all the discarded cell phones?

188
00:12:25,780 --> 00:12:29,080
Each day, 15,000 of them arrive here

189
00:12:29,081 --> 00:12:33,400
at a company called ReCellular near Ann Arbor, Michigan.

190
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:36,880
ReCellular is the world's center for used cell phones.

191
00:12:37,300 --> 00:12:41,040
We handle more used phones every day than any other company in the world.

192
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:46,920
We say every box and envelope is a mystery because we have no idea what model of phone someone might send us.

193
00:12:46,921 --> 00:12:54,240
So the first step is we have to open them up and identify which model of phone has been sent of the more than 500 that are in the marketplace right now.

194
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:58,600
After technicians identify each phone by its unique barcode,

195
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:02,520
The device undergoes special diagnostics using software

196
00:13:02,521 --> 00:13:05,480
developed by Recellular. The basic functionality of a

197
00:13:05,481 --> 00:13:07,480
phone is that it obviously has to power up.

198
00:13:07,481 --> 00:13:09,880
It needs to have a good LCD screen, and it needs to be able

199
00:13:09,881 --> 00:13:13,320
to make a call. If a phone is beyond repair

200
00:13:13,321 --> 00:13:16,760
or obsolete, it's sent to an outside company to be ground up

201
00:13:16,761 --> 00:13:21,160
and recycled. If the phone works, a technician

202
00:13:21,161 --> 00:13:26,920
wipes clean its memory chip. The phones are then repackaged,

203
00:13:26,921 --> 00:13:30,255
ready to be sold to budget-minded cell phone users in the U.S.

204
00:13:30,256 --> 00:13:31,600
and in foreign countries.

205
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:42,220
Today's feature-packed cell phones connect us in ways never imagined by the original inventor of the telephone.

206
00:13:43,080 --> 00:13:46,180
Think you know who that is? Well, think again.

207
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:54,440
On April 3rd, 1973, Martin Cooper of Motorola was the first person to talk on a cell phone.

208
00:13:54,441 --> 00:13:57,880
He made the historic call on the streets of New York City as

209
00:13:57,881 --> 00:14:01,080
startled onlookers gawked in disbelief.

210
00:14:05,080 --> 00:14:08,840
On June 11, 2002, the United States Congress issued a

211
00:14:08,841 --> 00:14:11,240
resolution honoring the inventor of the telephone.

212
00:14:11,241 --> 00:14:15,640
His name? Antonio Meucci, an Italian immigrant

213
00:14:15,641 --> 00:14:20,200
from New York City. Most people know Alexander Graham

214
00:14:20,201 --> 00:14:23,480
Bell as the inventor of the telephone, but reports say

215
00:14:23,481 --> 00:14:27,080
demonstrated an electromagnetic speech communication device he

216
00:14:27,081 --> 00:14:32,480
called the teletrophone in 1860, 26 years before Bell was

217
00:14:32,481 --> 00:14:36,720
credited with inventing the telephone.

218
00:14:36,721 --> 00:14:41,540
In 1871, Meucci suffered severe burns in a ferry boat accident,

219
00:14:41,541 --> 00:14:44,440
leaving him with neither the money nor the health to further

220
00:14:44,441 --> 00:14:48,480
exploit his invention. Despite recognition from

221
00:14:48,481 --> 00:14:52,040
Congress, not everyone agrees Meucci deserves the credit.

222
00:14:52,041 --> 00:14:53,540
The way that I think about Meucci

223
00:14:53,541 --> 00:14:56,540
is that there's a big difference between inventing something

224
00:14:56,541 --> 00:14:59,040
you can demonstrate in a workshop or a laboratory

225
00:14:59,041 --> 00:15:01,540
and something you can actually roll out commercially.

226
00:15:01,541 --> 00:15:04,040
It's certain that Meucci did not have a commercially practical

227
00:15:04,041 --> 00:15:07,040
telephone, even if he did have something that could have

228
00:15:07,041 --> 00:15:10,040
demonstrated some of the principles.

229
00:15:10,041 --> 00:15:13,040
The principles that eventually led to the first commercial

230
00:15:13,041 --> 00:15:16,040
telephones were based on a concept called undulating

231
00:15:16,041 --> 00:15:18,040
current.

232
00:15:18,041 --> 00:15:21,040
Several researchers of the time believed that the sound wave

233
00:15:21,041 --> 00:15:25,280
created by the human voice could be converted by a device into a

234
00:15:25,281 --> 00:15:29,780
continuous electrical wave, sent down wires, then reconverted

235
00:15:29,781 --> 00:15:35,380
into speech at the other end. By the mid-1870s, two

236
00:15:35,381 --> 00:15:39,500
inventors were racing neck and neck to patent such a device,

237
00:15:39,501 --> 00:15:44,400
Alexander Graham Bell of Boston and his Chicago rival, Elisha

238
00:15:44,401 --> 00:15:47,900
Gray. It would be Bell who would win

239
00:15:47,901 --> 00:15:50,900
the race. Well, this is a replica of the

240
00:15:50,901 --> 00:15:55,720
very first telephone, the so-called liquid transmitter that Alexander Graham Bell invented

241
00:15:55,721 --> 00:15:57,140
on March 10, 1876.

242
00:15:57,141 --> 00:16:02,820
And as the story goes, he yelled into the microphone or the transmitter,

243
00:16:03,380 --> 00:16:05,036
Mr. Watson, come here, I want you.

244
00:16:05,037 --> 00:16:07,520
And his assistant, Thomas Watson, on the other end,

245
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:12,200
heard that complete sentence. When Bell spoke into the cone,

246
00:16:12,201 --> 00:16:15,073
sound waves moved a parchment diaphragm.

247
00:16:15,074 --> 00:16:18,520
The diaphragm moved a thin metal rod up and down

248
00:16:18,521 --> 00:16:23,320
water that was made conductive by the addition of acid.

249
00:16:23,321 --> 00:16:26,520
The fluctuating electrical signal was sent along copper wires

250
00:16:26,521 --> 00:16:30,520
to a receiver, where the current caused an iron reed to vibrate,

251
00:16:30,521 --> 00:16:37,520
reproducing Bell's voice. Alexander Graham Bell filed a

252
00:16:37,521 --> 00:16:42,520
patent application for his telephone on February 14, 1876,

253
00:16:42,521 --> 00:16:45,520
purportedly two hours before Elisha Gray filed a preliminary

254
00:16:45,521 --> 00:16:49,120
a preliminary application called a caveat for his own telephone

255
00:16:49,121 --> 00:16:53,260
device. Three weeks later, Bell was

256
00:16:53,261 --> 00:16:56,680
officially granted the patent, but its issuance is still steeped

257
00:16:56,681 --> 00:16:59,620
in controversy. It turns out about 10 years

258
00:16:59,621 --> 00:17:03,120
later, the patent examiner in charge of electrical patent

259
00:17:03,121 --> 00:17:08,920
applications, a man named Major Zenas Wilber, testified that he

260
00:17:08,921 --> 00:17:12,580
had been bribed by Bell's patent attorney and Bell himself to the

261
00:17:12,581 --> 00:17:17,580
of $200 to predate Bell's application before Gray's,

262
00:17:17,581 --> 00:17:20,580
and also to show Bell Gray's application.

263
00:17:20,581 --> 00:17:22,580
So it's still an open question, at least in my mind,

264
00:17:22,581 --> 00:17:25,580
whether Bell or Gray should be properly credited

265
00:17:25,581 --> 00:17:27,580
as inventing the telephone.

266
00:17:27,581 --> 00:17:30,580
Armed with his patent, Bell and two financial backers

267
00:17:30,581 --> 00:17:32,580
set out to create the first telephone network

268
00:17:32,581 --> 00:17:34,580
in the United States.

269
00:17:34,581 --> 00:17:37,580
It came to be known as the Bell System.

270
00:17:37,581 --> 00:17:41,580
In 1899, American Telephone and Telegraph,

271
00:17:41,581 --> 00:17:47,580
or AT&T became the Bell Systems parent company.

272
00:17:47,581 --> 00:17:52,820
When Bell's patents expired in 1893 and 1894, more than 6,000

273
00:17:52,821 --> 00:17:56,820
independent phone companies sprang up around the country.

274
00:17:56,821 --> 00:17:59,820
Each ran their own set of phone lines, threatening to block out

275
00:17:59,821 --> 00:18:04,820
the sky in some cities. In an attempt to shore up its

276
00:18:04,821 --> 00:18:08,320
holdings, AT&T began purchasing some of the independent

277
00:18:08,321 --> 00:18:10,420
of the independent companies.

278
00:18:10,421 --> 00:18:13,120
This led to the federal government filing an antitrust

279
00:18:13,121 --> 00:18:17,500
lawsuit against AT&T in 1913.

280
00:18:17,501 --> 00:18:19,900
But Bell's company was ready for the fight.

281
00:18:19,901 --> 00:18:23,440
They said, if you're going to set up a nationwide telephone

282
00:18:23,441 --> 00:18:27,840
system, it doesn't make sense to set up two or three or five

283
00:18:27,841 --> 00:18:29,580
nationwide telephone systems.

284
00:18:29,581 --> 00:18:32,740
Telephone systems are expensive to build if you want to connect

285
00:18:32,741 --> 00:18:33,980
the entire country.

286
00:18:33,981 --> 00:18:36,720
So AT&T convinced the federal government that it really made

287
00:18:36,721 --> 00:18:40,960
to have one designated telephone company that provided universal

288
00:18:40,961 --> 00:18:42,660
service.

289
00:18:42,661 --> 00:18:45,460
AT&T agreed to accept federal regulation

290
00:18:45,461 --> 00:18:47,760
in exchange for being the dominant phone company

291
00:18:47,761 --> 00:18:52,760
in the United States. AT&T and its research arm,

292
00:18:52,761 --> 00:18:55,880
Bell Laboratories, then set out to complete a long-distance

293
00:18:55,881 --> 00:18:58,680
network that would connect cities across America.

294
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:03,480
Soon after the telephone's invention,

295
00:19:03,481 --> 00:19:07,680
small citywide networks called local exchanges quickly sprang up

296
00:19:07,681 --> 00:19:10,720
across the country. But there were no lines connecting

297
00:19:10,721 --> 00:19:16,220
one local exchange to another. In 1885, AT&T laid their first

298
00:19:16,221 --> 00:19:20,440
long-distance cable between New York and Philadelphia.

299
00:19:20,441 --> 00:19:23,440
Seven years later, it was possible to call from New York

300
00:19:23,441 --> 00:19:27,740
to Chicago. By 1915, you could call from New

301
00:19:27,741 --> 00:19:32,720
York to San Francisco. The entire coast-to-coast

302
00:19:32,721 --> 00:19:35,720
The coast-to-coast telephone network consisted of simple

303
00:19:35,721 --> 00:19:39,720
copper wires strung pole to pole or bound into cables and

304
00:19:39,721 --> 00:19:42,720
buried underground. When there was the need to

305
00:19:42,721 --> 00:19:45,720
bury the cable, they had to create cables like this, which

306
00:19:45,721 --> 00:19:48,720
are thin copper wire cables insulated from each other so

307
00:19:48,721 --> 00:19:51,720
there was no crosstalk and interference. A cable like this

308
00:19:51,721 --> 00:19:55,720
had the capacity of 600 to 900 simultaneous phone calls.

309
00:19:55,721 --> 00:19:58,720
The next big leap in technology was coaxial cable, which was in

310
00:19:58,721 --> 00:20:01,720
the 1940s. One single tube like this would have the same

311
00:20:01,721 --> 00:20:06,720
capacity as a 600 to 900 pair copper cable.

312
00:20:06,721 --> 00:20:09,720
When a telephone signal moves through copper wire,

313
00:20:09,721 --> 00:20:12,720
some of its energy dissipates from the wire and weakens the

314
00:20:12,721 --> 00:20:16,720
signal. Coaxial cable eliminates this

315
00:20:16,721 --> 00:20:20,720
dissipation by wrapping the wire in layers of insulation.

316
00:20:20,721 --> 00:20:23,720
A range of higher frequency signals can then be sent down

317
00:20:23,721 --> 00:20:28,720
the wire, allowing for far greater call capacity.

318
00:20:28,721 --> 00:20:31,720
The invention of coaxial technology led to the first

319
00:20:31,721 --> 00:20:35,160
transatlantic telephone cable, TAT-1.

320
00:20:35,260 --> 00:20:37,560
In the case of TAT-1, there were two cables,

321
00:20:37,660 --> 00:20:40,060
one for each direction of the conversation

322
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:41,360
that crossed the ocean.

323
00:20:41,460 --> 00:20:46,560
It had a capacity of about 36 simultaneous conversations.

324
00:20:46,660 --> 00:20:51,080
AT&T opened TAT-1 for business in 1956.

325
00:20:51,180 --> 00:20:54,080
Today, there are more than a dozen transatlantic cables,

326
00:20:54,180 --> 00:20:56,640
including fiber optic lines that can handle millions

327
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:57,980
of simultaneous calls.

328
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:05,720
Once telephony conquered both land and sea, the only way to go

329
00:21:05,820 --> 00:21:07,220
was up.

330
00:21:07,320 --> 00:21:13,060
On July 11, 1962, AT&T's Telstar, the world's first active

331
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:16,160
telecommunications satellite, blasted into orbit.

332
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:24,200
Telstar received signals beamed up from AT&T's ground station

333
00:21:24,300 --> 00:21:26,280
in Andover, Maine.

334
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:30,120
The signals were amplified over 10,000 times by the satellite's

335
00:21:30,121 --> 00:21:33,460
processors, then beamed back down to one of two receiving

336
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:38,120
stations in Europe. Today, a fully functional backup of

337
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:41,500
the original Telstar hangs in the lobby of Bell Labs New

338
00:21:41,560 --> 00:21:44,960
Jersey headquarters. The solar cell had just been

339
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:48,700
invented in Bell Laboratories. The actual surface of the 34

340
00:21:48,780 --> 00:21:53,740
inch diameter Telstar satellite is covered in 3,600 solar cells.

341
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:58,620
So that provided the power. As its network expanded to

342
00:21:58,621 --> 00:22:02,220
to encircle the globe, AT&T became the largest corporation

343
00:22:02,221 --> 00:22:06,620
on Earth. By 1974, it employed 1 million

344
00:22:06,621 --> 00:22:12,620
people and controlled 80% of the US telephone market.

345
00:22:12,621 --> 00:22:16,620
That same year, federal antitrust regulators once again sought

346
00:22:16,621 --> 00:22:20,620
to break up AT&T. In 1982, the company agreed to

347
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:27,620
divest 75% of its assets. AT&T's 22 wholly owned Bell

348
00:22:27,621 --> 00:22:30,820
telephone companies were reorganized into seven fully

349
00:22:30,821 --> 00:22:34,820
independent regional companies known as Baby Bells.

350
00:22:34,821 --> 00:22:37,860
The idea was the Baby Bells would provide local phone

351
00:22:37,861 --> 00:22:41,860
service, whereas AT&T would compete in the long-distance

352
00:22:41,861 --> 00:22:45,700
market. As the telephone network

353
00:22:45,701 --> 00:22:49,380
matured from copper wire strung from holes to signals beamed

354
00:22:49,381 --> 00:22:52,420
through outer space, the phone instrument underwent its own

355
00:22:52,421 --> 00:22:56,420
evolution. The first commercially available

356
00:22:56,421 --> 00:22:58,820
telephones were housed in wooden cabinets.

357
00:22:58,821 --> 00:23:02,560
The phone I'm holding here is an 1896 Western Electric common

358
00:23:02,561 --> 00:23:05,560
battery phone. And so as soon as you picked up

359
00:23:05,561 --> 00:23:08,460
the receiver, it would signal the operator.

360
00:23:08,461 --> 00:23:10,860
The telephone operator would know that someone was trying to

361
00:23:10,861 --> 00:23:12,980
connect a call, and she would say, number, please, and she

362
00:23:12,981 --> 00:23:16,980
would connect the circuit. At the dawn of the 20th

363
00:23:16,981 --> 00:23:20,520
century, sleek candlestick phones made of nickel were all

364
00:23:20,521 --> 00:23:23,520
the rage. These are the first desk sets,

365
00:23:23,521 --> 00:23:27,120
telephones that were offered to subscribers.

366
00:23:27,121 --> 00:23:30,120
After you've placed the call, you've talked to your friend,

367
00:23:30,121 --> 00:23:33,960
you would place the receiver onto the switch hook, and that's

368
00:23:33,961 --> 00:23:37,760
called hanging up. That's where we got the term from.

369
00:23:37,761 --> 00:23:41,380
In the 1920s, Bell Telephone designers married the earpiece

370
00:23:41,381 --> 00:23:46,380
and the mouthpiece into one unit, called a combined handset.

371
00:23:46,381 --> 00:23:49,280
This model included what would become the workhorse of the

372
00:23:49,281 --> 00:23:56,080
system, the 500 series phone. The 500 debuted in 1949 and was

373
00:23:56,081 --> 00:24:00,480
still being manufactured up until 1983.

374
00:24:00,481 --> 00:24:05,680
At the 1962 Seattle World's Fair, AT&T showcased a new

375
00:24:05,681 --> 00:24:10,480
space-age technology that would make the rotary dial obsolete,

376
00:24:10,481 --> 00:24:16,480
the touchtone telephone. Touchtone used special tones

377
00:24:16,481 --> 00:24:19,880
that could not only dial numbers, but also operate answering

378
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:22,480
machines and computers through the telephone network.

379
00:24:25,280 --> 00:24:28,720
Over its 130-year evolution, the telephone has enabled

380
00:24:28,820 --> 00:24:31,920
interpersonal communication to an extent barely dreamed of

381
00:24:32,020 --> 00:24:35,440
by its creators. But perhaps one of the telephone's

382
00:24:35,540 --> 00:24:40,140
greatest contributions has been its ability to help save lives.

383
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:43,040
Los Angeles County Guard Department.

384
00:24:43,041 --> 00:24:47,040
In 1878, Rutherford B. Hayes was the first U.S. president

385
00:24:47,140 --> 00:24:49,680
to have a telephone installed in the White House.

386
00:24:49,780 --> 00:24:53,080
The president placed his first call to Alexander Graham Bell,

387
00:24:53,180 --> 00:24:56,780
and his first words asked Bell to speak more slowly.

388
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:05,400
In the United States, three simple numbers dialed

389
00:25:05,500 --> 00:25:09,300
on any phone can mean the difference between life or death.

390
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:11,500
911.

391
00:25:11,501 --> 00:25:13,500
Los Angeles County Fire Department.

392
00:25:13,501 --> 00:25:15,500
And what is your emergency right now?

393
00:25:15,501 --> 00:25:17,500
One of the wonderful things about being able to pick up a

394
00:25:17,501 --> 00:25:21,500
telephone and call anyone at any time is in emergency situations

395
00:25:21,501 --> 00:25:24,500
this wasn't possible, of course, with older forms of

396
00:25:24,501 --> 00:25:27,500
communications like the telegraph, or before the telegraph,

397
00:25:27,501 --> 00:25:30,500
it wasn't possible at all.

398
00:25:30,501 --> 00:25:34,500
Depending on where you live, dialing 911 might connect you

399
00:25:34,501 --> 00:25:38,500
to just one person manning a regular telephone, or to a state

400
00:25:38,501 --> 00:25:41,540
or to a state-of-the-art emergency dispatch center,

401
00:25:41,541 --> 00:25:44,180
like this one operated by the Los Angeles County Fire

402
00:25:44,181 --> 00:25:46,380
Department.

403
00:25:46,381 --> 00:25:50,680
Operators here handle an average of 1,400 calls every day.

404
00:25:50,681 --> 00:25:53,720
Now you said you're having chest pain.

405
00:25:53,721 --> 00:25:57,680
Los Angeles County opened its 32,000 square foot 911

406
00:25:57,681 --> 00:26:01,760
center in 1991. What is your emergency?

407
00:26:01,761 --> 00:26:05,400
Trouble breathing? The calls arrive

408
00:26:05,401 --> 00:26:08,000
at the center digitally encrypted with a code

409
00:26:08,001 --> 00:26:12,300
indicating in what part of the county it originated.

410
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:16,540
Los Angeles County utilizes a system known as E911,

411
00:26:16,640 --> 00:26:22,240
or Enhanced 911. E911 accesses a computer database

412
00:26:22,340 --> 00:26:24,820
that automatically gives the operator the physical address

413
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:27,120
of the caller. OK, let me confirm the address

414
00:26:27,220 --> 00:26:29,120
is 1320 Northeastern Avenue in Los Angeles.

415
00:26:29,220 --> 00:26:33,560
Is that correct? With one press of a key,

416
00:26:33,660 --> 00:26:36,020
the call taker sends the nature of the emergency

417
00:26:36,021 --> 00:26:40,060
and the caller's address to a nearby radio dispatcher.

418
00:26:40,160 --> 00:26:42,400
The radio dispatcher's computer automatically

419
00:26:42,500 --> 00:26:45,300
determines the closest fire station to the emergency.

420
00:26:45,400 --> 00:26:47,400
Angel one, squad three, chest pain.

421
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:49,940
1320 North Eastern Avenue.

422
00:26:50,040 --> 00:26:53,200
The computer will then send out a digital signal

423
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:55,840
through the airwaves, and it will then

424
00:26:55,940 --> 00:27:00,020
be received at the fire stations with all the actual dispatch

425
00:27:00,120 --> 00:27:03,520
information as far as the location and type of emergency

426
00:27:03,620 --> 00:27:06,020
that they are being sent to.

427
00:27:06,021 --> 00:27:13,020
The US Congress mandated a universal 911 system in 1968.

428
00:27:13,021 --> 00:27:17,020
The number was chosen because it fit with other service numbers

429
00:27:17,021 --> 00:27:22,020
like 411 for information and 611 for repairs.

430
00:27:22,021 --> 00:27:27,020
Today, approximately 96% of the geographic US is covered by some

431
00:27:27,021 --> 00:27:32,020
type of 911 service. In the year 2000, the system logged

432
00:27:32,021 --> 00:27:36,660
the system logged approximately 150 million calls and saved

433
00:27:36,661 --> 00:27:40,660
countless lives. But as more and more people adopt

434
00:27:40,661 --> 00:27:44,660
cell phones as their primary telephone, 911 centers have a

435
00:27:44,661 --> 00:27:48,980
more difficult time ascertaining a caller's location.

436
00:27:50,980 --> 00:27:54,980
Older cell phones using older networks can only be traced to

437
00:27:54,981 --> 00:27:58,580
the nearest cell tower. Since towers can be 8 or as far as

438
00:27:58,581 --> 00:28:02,340
far as 20 miles apart, this only offers a rough idea of the

439
00:28:02,341 --> 00:28:06,580
cell phone's location. Newer cell phones can be tracked

440
00:28:06,581 --> 00:28:10,580
using network-based triangulation. The strength of

441
00:28:10,581 --> 00:28:13,580
the cell phone's signal can be measured between three or four

442
00:28:13,581 --> 00:28:16,580
cell towers, allowing for an accuracy of within two football

443
00:28:16,581 --> 00:28:20,580
fields. The most precise method involves

444
00:28:20,581 --> 00:28:25,580
GPS technology. GPS, short for Global Positioning

445
00:28:25,581 --> 00:28:29,120
for Global Positioning System utilizes a constellation

446
00:28:29,121 --> 00:28:31,460
of satellites to triangulate the location

447
00:28:31,461 --> 00:28:34,220
of a GPS-enabled phone.

448
00:28:34,221 --> 00:28:36,500
Roughly half of the 200 million cell phones

449
00:28:36,501 --> 00:28:39,720
in the United States are currently GPS-enabled.

450
00:28:39,721 --> 00:28:43,640
Prepare to turn right in 0.3 miles.

451
00:28:43,641 --> 00:28:46,260
If you have the newer technology cell phones

452
00:28:46,261 --> 00:28:51,140
with the GPS chips, we can pinpoint you right on the map

453
00:28:51,141 --> 00:28:53,900
regardless of where you are located.

454
00:28:56,980 --> 00:29:00,380
At Lucent Bell Labs in Murray Hill, New Jersey,

455
00:29:00,381 --> 00:29:03,300
researchers have developed a new cell phone tracking service

456
00:29:03,301 --> 00:29:07,260
called iLocator. iLocator allows a subscriber

457
00:29:07,261 --> 00:29:10,460
to pinpoint the exact location of another GPS-enabled cell

458
00:29:10,461 --> 00:29:12,420
phone.

459
00:29:12,421 --> 00:29:14,580
As long as the cell phone is kept on,

460
00:29:14,581 --> 00:29:17,260
iLocator can help parents keep track of where their children

461
00:29:17,261 --> 00:29:20,760
are at any given moment, or help first responders locate

462
00:29:20,761 --> 00:29:23,760
in the aftermath of a disaster.

463
00:29:23,761 --> 00:29:26,760
In scenarios where people have their cell phones

464
00:29:26,761 --> 00:29:30,760
but are unable to talk and they are trapped in buildings,

465
00:29:30,761 --> 00:29:33,760
public safety officials can use location-based technologies

466
00:29:33,761 --> 00:29:37,760
to locate where these people are.

467
00:29:37,761 --> 00:29:39,760
The next generation of location-based technology

468
00:29:39,761 --> 00:29:45,760
will enable a user to set up what's called a geographic fence.

469
00:29:45,761 --> 00:29:48,760
As demonstrated here on Microsoft's Virtual Earth,

470
00:29:48,761 --> 00:29:51,660
virtual earth. When a specified GPS-enabled

471
00:29:51,661 --> 00:29:55,800
cell phone either enters or leaves the fence, the network will

472
00:29:55,801 --> 00:29:58,500
automatically send a message to the user's cell phone.

473
00:29:58,501 --> 00:30:02,020
Let's say I'm the parent. I'm the dad, and I want to create

474
00:30:02,021 --> 00:30:06,060
a geographic fence saying, well, whenever my son enters, say, the

475
00:30:06,061 --> 00:30:08,960
34th Street and Park Avenue, I would like to receive a message

476
00:30:08,961 --> 00:30:13,000
saying that he reached there safely. This icon represents a

477
00:30:13,001 --> 00:30:15,860
cell phone moving towards the designated geographic fence.

478
00:30:15,861 --> 00:30:18,860
The moment the phone crosses the fence...

479
00:30:18,861 --> 00:30:22,860
So my phone just went off, and this basically shows that I got

480
00:30:22,861 --> 00:30:26,860
a text message containing exactly the text we typed in,

481
00:30:26,861 --> 00:30:28,860
saying Rohan has reached home safe.

482
00:30:28,861 --> 00:30:31,860
Although cell phone users must grant permission to allow

483
00:30:31,861 --> 00:30:34,860
their phones to be tracked by iLocator, the fact that it's

484
00:30:34,861 --> 00:30:39,860
possible at all raises issues about personal privacy.

485
00:30:39,861 --> 00:30:42,860
As we enter a new era of increased electronic

486
00:30:42,861 --> 00:30:45,260
increased electronic surveillance.

487
00:30:45,261 --> 00:30:47,560
Some people are taking extra measures

488
00:30:47,561 --> 00:30:49,460
to make sure no one is listening in.

489
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:55,240
When the 911 system was originally introduced,

490
00:30:55,241 --> 00:30:58,040
it was promoted as 911 service.

491
00:30:58,041 --> 00:31:00,820
After some panicked callers tried to find the 11 key

492
00:31:00,821 --> 00:31:04,020
on their telephones, it was changed to 911.

