00:31:08,180 --> 00:31:11,320
As part of the War on Terror, President Bush

494
00:31:11,321 --> 00:31:14,560
National Security Agency to monitor telephone calls in the

495
00:31:14,561 --> 00:31:18,120
U.S. without obtaining a court order.

496
00:31:18,121 --> 00:31:21,460
When the secret program was revealed by the New York Times,

497
00:31:21,461 --> 00:31:24,260
it raised concerns about the average American's right to

498
00:31:24,261 --> 00:31:28,580
privacy. But the truth is, you don't have

499
00:31:28,581 --> 00:31:31,180
to be the long arm of the government to listen in on a

500
00:31:31,181 --> 00:31:33,980
phone call. Illegal eavesdropping by

501
00:31:33,981 --> 00:31:37,620
corporate spies or private investigators is easier than you

502
00:31:37,621 --> 00:31:41,320
might imagine.

503
00:31:41,321 --> 00:31:43,320
Rick Hoffman is in the technical surveillance

504
00:31:43,420 --> 00:31:48,120
countermeasures business, or TSCM for short.

505
00:31:48,220 --> 00:31:52,160
He fights illegal wiretappers.

506
00:31:52,260 --> 00:31:55,260
Rick utilizes an arsenal of sophisticated equipment

507
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:57,160
to look for electronic devices that

508
00:31:57,260 --> 00:32:00,280
can be tapped into a phone line to transmit or record

509
00:32:00,380 --> 00:32:01,280
your conversations.

510
00:32:04,380 --> 00:32:06,680
The most vulnerable spot in any residence

511
00:32:06,780 --> 00:32:09,720
is the exterior phone box, where the phone lines enter

512
00:32:09,721 --> 00:32:13,820
lines enter the house. We have the telephone network

513
00:32:13,821 --> 00:32:20,150
interface box in the corner here. No special locks, no special tools to open it.

514
00:32:20,151 --> 00:32:21,020
Open it up.

515
00:32:21,021 --> 00:32:28,220
This is the subscriber line where the resident's phone lines come in, and these two screws right

516
00:32:28,221 --> 00:32:31,966
here are where a wire man would attach a wiretap.

517
00:32:31,967 --> 00:32:35,560
If no devices are found here, Rick will conduct

518
00:32:35,561 --> 00:32:39,060
a thorough scan of the inside of the residence.

519
00:32:39,160 --> 00:32:42,100
A nonlinear junction detector picks up signals given off

520
00:32:42,200 --> 00:32:47,900
by components, often found in voice transmitters.

521
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:49,600
Even the telephone instrument itself

522
00:32:49,700 --> 00:32:52,220
can be hiding a transmitter.

523
00:32:52,320 --> 00:32:56,320
Instead of taking it apart, Rick uses x-rays to peer inside.

524
00:32:56,420 --> 00:32:58,820
This is a battery-powered portable x-ray,

525
00:32:58,920 --> 00:33:01,120
also known as a fluoroscope.

526
00:33:01,220 --> 00:33:03,520
And having been in the countermeasures business

527
00:33:03,620 --> 00:33:05,560
for a number of years, I am familiar with what

528
00:33:05,561 --> 00:33:08,560
is supposed to look like so I can tell if there's something

529
00:33:08,561 --> 00:33:12,560
that doesn't belong there.

530
00:33:12,561 --> 00:33:15,560
And this phone is clear.

531
00:33:15,561 --> 00:33:18,560
A wiretapper can easily tap a phone by attaching a radio

532
00:33:18,561 --> 00:33:22,560
transmitter to the phone line.

533
00:33:22,561 --> 00:33:25,560
Transmitters automatically turn on when a phone is taken off

534
00:33:25,561 --> 00:33:28,560
the hook. This is a radio frequency

535
00:33:28,561 --> 00:33:32,560
spectrum analyzer. And if there was a transmitter attached to

536
00:33:32,561 --> 00:33:35,560
to this particular telephone, if I raise the handset,

537
00:33:35,561 --> 00:33:39,360
we should see a pretty decent spike in this particular area.

538
00:33:39,361 --> 00:33:43,600
And sure enough, there is a spike.

539
00:33:43,601 --> 00:33:47,080
The spike was caused by this seemingly ordinary modular

540
00:33:47,081 --> 00:33:50,480
adapter, which is actually a black market device that has a

541
00:33:50,481 --> 00:33:54,520
transmitter built inside. Rick placed it on the line to

542
00:33:54,521 --> 00:33:57,520
demonstrate how the frequency spectrum analyzer works.

543
00:33:57,521 --> 00:34:00,520
This one is for training purposes only.

544
00:34:00,521 --> 00:34:02,020
It does not transmit audio.

545
00:34:02,021 --> 00:34:05,420
It's strictly for me to help train my technicians.

546
00:34:05,421 --> 00:34:08,120
Had this been a real transmitter,

547
00:34:08,121 --> 00:34:10,520
it would have transmitted both sides of the telephone

548
00:34:10,521 --> 00:34:14,760
conversation up to several blocks away,

549
00:34:14,761 --> 00:34:16,760
under the exact perfect conditions,

550
00:34:16,761 --> 00:34:19,880
maybe even a mile or more away.

551
00:34:19,881 --> 00:34:21,980
Whether legal or illegal,

552
00:34:21,981 --> 00:34:24,280
electronic eavesdropping has been in practice

553
00:34:24,281 --> 00:34:27,020
since the birth of the telephone.

554
00:34:27,021 --> 00:34:29,520
In the early days, all the wiretapper could do

555
00:34:29,521 --> 00:34:33,260
was physically splice into a phone line, listen in, and take

556
00:34:33,261 --> 00:34:36,760
notes. Later, the ability to record

557
00:34:36,761 --> 00:34:39,360
phone conversations developed along with sound recording

558
00:34:39,361 --> 00:34:43,960
technology of the day, from pre-World War II wire

559
00:34:43,961 --> 00:34:48,480
recorders, which captured sound on a thin steel wire, to the

560
00:34:48,481 --> 00:34:52,580
first magnetic tape recorders of the late 1940s.

561
00:34:52,581 --> 00:34:57,480
The invention of the transistor by Bell Labs in 1947 allowed

562
00:34:57,481 --> 00:35:00,680
for the miniaturization of transmitters and amplifiers,

563
00:35:00,681 --> 00:35:04,380
which could then be hidden directly inside telephones.

564
00:35:04,381 --> 00:35:08,020
The introduction of the microprocessor in the 1970s

565
00:35:08,021 --> 00:35:11,620
added even smaller devices to the wireman's arsenal.

566
00:35:11,621 --> 00:35:15,040
It's now possible to hide a transmitter in something this

567
00:35:15,041 --> 00:35:17,540
small.

568
00:35:17,541 --> 00:35:19,940
Currently, the utmost in phone privacy

569
00:35:19,941 --> 00:35:23,140
can be achieved by using your cell phone.

570
00:35:23,141 --> 00:35:26,080
Today's digitally encoded wireless networks are virtually

571
00:35:26,081 --> 00:35:29,780
impossible to listen in on. That is, without specialized

572
00:35:29,781 --> 00:35:34,320
equipment costing half a million dollars or more.

573
00:35:34,321 --> 00:35:37,820
Such equipment, like any wiretap device, is illegal to possess in

574
00:35:37,821 --> 00:35:40,820
the United States. Even devices that we find

575
00:35:40,821 --> 00:35:44,140
are immediately turned over to law enforcement.

576
00:35:44,141 --> 00:35:48,040
It is a federal felony to possess one.

577
00:35:48,041 --> 00:35:51,040
Up until recently, it was also a federal felony to possess

578
00:35:51,041 --> 00:35:54,480
one of these phone tampering devices, on display at the

579
00:35:54,481 --> 00:35:59,580
at the Computer History Museum in Mountain View, California.

580
00:35:59,581 --> 00:36:04,120
Although it's black in color, it's known as a blue box.

581
00:36:04,121 --> 00:36:07,820
Now obsolete, blue boxes once allowed users to freely explore

582
00:36:07,821 --> 00:36:12,440
the phone network and get free long distance in the bargain.

583
00:36:12,441 --> 00:36:15,740
What makes this blue box worthy of display is that it was built

584
00:36:15,741 --> 00:36:19,740
by Steve Wozniak, who along with Steve Jobs was the co-founder

585
00:36:19,741 --> 00:36:24,320
of Apple Computer. The users of blue boxes were known

586
00:36:24,321 --> 00:36:25,860
as phone freakers.

587
00:36:25,960 --> 00:36:27,860
It was a bunch of brilliant engineers

588
00:36:27,960 --> 00:36:30,360
that had built little devices that explored cracks

589
00:36:30,460 --> 00:36:32,960
in the phone system, and they had these devices that could

590
00:36:33,060 --> 00:36:35,860
put normal tones into any telephone

591
00:36:35,960 --> 00:36:38,060
and cause the whole phone networks of the world

592
00:36:38,160 --> 00:36:40,800
to switch around, send calls on satellites and everything,

593
00:36:40,900 --> 00:36:42,240
all for free.

594
00:36:42,340 --> 00:36:44,700
An anonymous computer engineer, later revealed

595
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:48,440
to be John T. Draper, was phone freaking's most notorious

596
00:36:48,540 --> 00:36:49,680
adherent.

597
00:36:49,780 --> 00:36:53,380
Draper went by the code name Captain Crunch.

598
00:36:53,381 --> 00:36:54,720
Captain Crunch got his name.

599
00:36:54,820 --> 00:36:57,620
He got this little toy whistle out of a box of cereal,

600
00:36:57,720 --> 00:36:59,380
the same as a lot of us did.

601
00:36:59,480 --> 00:37:03,820
And somehow he or other phone freaks discovered

602
00:37:03,920 --> 00:37:06,900
that the tone it puts out includes a lot of this one

603
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:09,360
pitch, one particular pitch that if you're

604
00:37:09,460 --> 00:37:12,200
on a long-distance phone call, seizes the phone call

605
00:37:12,300 --> 00:37:13,660
with a chinking sound.

606
00:37:13,760 --> 00:37:17,500
And now the phone company is waiting obediently for tones

607
00:37:17,600 --> 00:37:18,700
to tell it where to dial.

608
00:37:18,800 --> 00:37:21,800
And this is the start of it all.

609
00:37:21,801 --> 00:37:24,800
The 2600 hertz tone produced by the whistle

610
00:37:24,900 --> 00:37:27,940
reset the multi-frequency long-distance switch,

611
00:37:28,040 --> 00:37:29,800
which was like opening up a secret door

612
00:37:29,900 --> 00:37:32,240
into the phone network.

613
00:37:32,340 --> 00:37:35,240
Once inside, a phone freaker could use a blue box

614
00:37:35,340 --> 00:37:37,540
to route a call anywhere in the world.

615
00:37:37,640 --> 00:37:41,060
One of the times, I said, just as a make-up joke,

616
00:37:41,160 --> 00:37:42,460
we could call the pope.

617
00:37:42,560 --> 00:37:43,560
Sure.

618
00:37:43,660 --> 00:37:46,400
So I called Italy inward and asked for Rome inward,

619
00:37:46,500 --> 00:37:48,060
and I got to the Vatican inward.

620
00:37:48,160 --> 00:37:51,000
And the Vatican operator, I said, this is Henry Kissinger.

621
00:37:51,001 --> 00:37:52,160
I didn't even use an accent.

622
00:37:52,161 --> 00:37:53,460
I said, this is Henry Kissinger.

623
00:37:53,461 --> 00:37:55,040
I'm calling on behalf of President Nixon

624
00:37:55,041 --> 00:37:56,380
at the summit meeting in Moscow.

625
00:37:56,381 --> 00:37:58,000
We'd like to talk to the Pope.

626
00:37:58,001 --> 00:37:59,420
They said, oh, it's 530 here.

627
00:37:59,421 --> 00:38:00,720
He's not awake yet.

628
00:38:00,721 --> 00:38:01,560
So call back later.

629
00:38:01,561 --> 00:38:02,880
So I called back in an hour.

630
00:38:02,881 --> 00:38:04,560
And they put me all the way through to the bishop, who

631
00:38:04,561 --> 00:38:06,060
was going to be the translator.

632
00:38:06,061 --> 00:38:07,940
And I used a Henry Kissinger type voice.

633
00:38:07,941 --> 00:38:09,600
I said, this is Henry Kissinger.

634
00:38:09,601 --> 00:38:11,680
And he says, I just spoke to Henry Kissinger.

635
00:38:11,681 --> 00:38:14,300
So I guess they called Russia, called Moscow,

636
00:38:14,301 --> 00:38:16,880
to check up on my story.

637
00:38:16,881 --> 00:38:19,640
All multi-frequency trunk lines in the continental United

638
00:38:19,641 --> 00:38:22,640
have now been replaced, rendering Captain Crunch whistles

639
00:38:22,641 --> 00:38:27,140
and blue boxes obsolete as freaking devices.

640
00:38:27,141 --> 00:38:29,440
For me, it was really a world of exploration.

641
00:38:29,441 --> 00:38:31,140
It was really trying to extend the state of the art

642
00:38:31,141 --> 00:38:33,140
and see how much new you can do that's even weirder.

643
00:38:33,141 --> 00:38:35,140
And you know what? You run out of things to do,

644
00:38:35,141 --> 00:38:39,140
and then it gets boring. Around the same time

645
00:38:39,141 --> 00:38:43,140
Steve Wozniak was building blue boxes, AT&T was introducing

646
00:38:43,141 --> 00:38:47,140
America to a new service that was supposed to revolutionize

647
00:38:47,141 --> 00:38:52,140
telephony, the picture phone. Today, one innovator is putting

648
00:38:52,141 --> 00:38:59,140
a new spin on that old idea. In 2005, law enforcement

649
00:38:59,141 --> 00:39:03,140
authorities in the United States performed roughly 1,800 wire

650
00:39:03,141 --> 00:39:06,140
taps, not including taps made by intelligence gathering

651
00:39:06,141 --> 00:39:13,140
agencies. If one inventor gets his way,

652
00:39:13,141 --> 00:39:16,140
this is how we'll be communicating in the future.

653
00:39:16,141 --> 00:39:18,300
Hi, Brent. Hey, Jennifer.

654
00:39:20,300 --> 00:39:23,880
Meet Sparky, a robotic video phone prototype

655
00:39:23,881 --> 00:39:25,960
cobbled together from simple technologies.

656
00:39:27,240 --> 00:39:31,680
As seen in this demonstration, Sparky uses two-way video cameras

657
00:39:31,681 --> 00:39:35,440
and a radio controller to allow a person to be in one location

658
00:39:35,441 --> 00:39:39,120
while moving around and interacting with people in a separate location.

659
00:39:40,740 --> 00:39:44,760
Sparky is, I believe, a natural extension of what the promise

660
00:39:44,761 --> 00:39:47,000
of the telephone was originally.

661
00:39:47,001 --> 00:39:49,760
People are often very surprised by how comfortable they are

662
00:39:49,761 --> 00:39:51,200
interacting with Sparky.

663
00:39:51,201 --> 00:39:52,940
And before they know it, they're

664
00:39:52,941 --> 00:39:55,840
having this intimate one-on-one conversation,

665
00:39:55,841 --> 00:39:57,800
and they practically forget the fact

666
00:39:57,801 --> 00:40:01,340
that they're really talking to a 300-pound metal device,

667
00:40:01,341 --> 00:40:05,080
and they talk to the person like the person is right there.

668
00:40:05,081 --> 00:40:06,580
Hi, Frank.

669
00:40:06,581 --> 00:40:09,020
Hey, Jennifer.

670
00:40:09,021 --> 00:40:11,680
Version 2.0 of Sparky will be encased

671
00:40:11,681 --> 00:40:14,420
in a sleek shell and operable over the internet

672
00:40:14,421 --> 00:40:20,260
via Wi-Fi. Sparky is an updated mobile version

673
00:40:20,261 --> 00:40:24,980
of the original picture phone. AT&T debuted their space age

674
00:40:24,981 --> 00:40:29,320
wonder at the 1964 World's Fair and trotted out commercial

675
00:40:29,321 --> 00:40:33,760
service in 1970. But the picture phone never

676
00:40:33,761 --> 00:40:38,520
caught on. Mark believes his updated version

677
00:40:38,521 --> 00:40:41,820
would work well as a museum guide or used in a classroom

678
00:40:41,821 --> 00:40:44,620
student or teacher can't be at school.

679
00:40:44,720 --> 00:40:46,060
Good morning, class.

680
00:40:46,160 --> 00:40:48,460
My name is Mrs. Townsend, and I will be your substitute

681
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:50,860
teacher today.

682
00:40:50,960 --> 00:40:54,360
It's a concept known as distance learning.

683
00:40:54,460 --> 00:40:57,560
And Robert Miller of AT&T thinks that the next generation

684
00:40:57,660 --> 00:41:00,380
wireless network will make it a reality.

685
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:04,680
Distance learning is an extension of the old-fashioned

686
00:41:04,780 --> 00:41:10,520
school, where what you're learning may be miles and miles

687
00:41:10,521 --> 00:41:15,220
miles away from an expert or a repository of knowledge.

688
00:41:15,221 --> 00:41:18,820
And I think robots' virtual presence

689
00:41:18,821 --> 00:41:21,740
are ways of allowing the human mind

690
00:41:21,741 --> 00:41:24,040
to extend itself over distance.

691
00:41:24,041 --> 00:41:28,080
And I think that's the real power of modern communication.

692
00:41:30,840 --> 00:41:34,780
At NASA Ames Research in Moffett Field, California,

693
00:41:34,781 --> 00:41:37,640
researchers have upped the ante on how people might one day

694
00:41:37,641 --> 00:41:42,040
communicate with each other and with machines.

695
00:41:42,041 --> 00:41:45,280
These robotic rovers are being controlled with a communications

696
00:41:45,281 --> 00:41:50,160
method that seems more like telepathy than telephony.

697
00:41:50,161 --> 00:41:53,080
NASA calls it sub-vocal speech.

698
00:41:53,081 --> 00:41:56,000
By use of special sensors that interpret muscle signals in the

699
00:41:56,001 --> 00:41:59,160
larynx and tongue, these technicians are able to maneuver

700
00:41:59,161 --> 00:42:03,900
the rovers by silently voicing certain commands.

701
00:42:03,901 --> 00:42:06,800
What's going on here is sometimes confused with people

702
00:42:06,801 --> 00:42:07,800
your thoughts.

703
00:42:07,801 --> 00:42:09,700
We're not really reading your thoughts.

704
00:42:09,701 --> 00:42:11,800
We're reading the result of a conscious decision

705
00:42:11,801 --> 00:42:13,300
to say a word.

706
00:42:13,301 --> 00:42:16,700
So what's happening here is Pavel is thinking of a word.

707
00:42:16,701 --> 00:42:19,440
And as he thinks of that word, it's transformed

708
00:42:19,441 --> 00:42:23,240
into a series of signals that are going down his nervous system

709
00:42:23,241 --> 00:42:27,660
to his vocal tract, either in his throat or under his tongue.

710
00:42:27,661 --> 00:42:32,060
These signals are being picked up and displayed on the screen.

711
00:42:32,061 --> 00:42:34,160
A computer programmed to recognize

712
00:42:34,161 --> 00:42:37,860
the signals as individual words can then send those words via

713
00:42:37,861 --> 00:42:41,760
a wireless network to a machine like a robot or to a handheld

714
00:42:41,761 --> 00:42:43,060
device.

715
00:42:43,061 --> 00:42:45,400
Fire clear.

716
00:42:45,401 --> 00:42:48,200
NASA is developing sub-vocal technology for use

717
00:42:48,201 --> 00:42:52,720
by astronauts, firefighters, soldiers in battle, and others

718
00:42:52,721 --> 00:42:56,120
who work in noisy environments where speech communication may

719
00:42:56,121 --> 00:42:57,420
be difficult.

720
00:42:57,421 --> 00:43:00,220
Personally, I'm excited about the possibility that we may

721
00:43:00,221 --> 00:43:03,020
enable a whole new way for humans to communicate with

722
00:43:03,021 --> 00:43:05,780
communicate with their world.

723
00:43:05,781 --> 00:43:08,260
With technology that reads signals sent directly

724
00:43:08,261 --> 00:43:12,060
from the brain, how long will it be before we create telephones

725
00:43:12,061 --> 00:43:15,060
that can be embedded directly into our heads?

726
00:43:15,061 --> 00:43:18,260
Would you want a cell phone implanted inside your tooth?

727
00:43:18,261 --> 00:43:21,380
No matter how small they get, there's still a particular

728
00:43:21,381 --> 00:43:25,380
comfort level in being able to hold a device as opposed to

729
00:43:25,381 --> 00:43:28,180
having one implanted inside you.

730
00:43:28,181 --> 00:43:31,180
So I think people will always want to have the sensation of

731
00:43:31,181 --> 00:43:33,420
by holding something.

732
00:43:33,520 --> 00:43:35,920
And that something is the telephone.

733
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:38,820
I think our world, due to communication,

734
00:43:38,920 --> 00:43:41,220
is becoming a smaller place.

735
00:43:41,320 --> 00:43:46,220
And the knowledge that we share is becoming so dispersed

736
00:43:46,320 --> 00:43:50,540
that we need a good network to pull together

737
00:43:50,640 --> 00:43:52,040
the community of humans.

738
00:43:52,140 --> 00:43:55,340
I think in the end, it will probably keep us

739
00:43:55,440 --> 00:43:58,540
from blowing ourselves up.

740
00:43:58,640 --> 00:44:00,040
Saving the world.

741
00:44:00,041 --> 00:44:04,540
Not bad for a device whose first use was to call for assistance.

